<h1 class="prose">Temperature, and the affine rules</h1>
<p class="prose">°C and °F are offset scales, so not every operation on them is meaningful.</p>
<p class="prose">A reading on an offset scale is a point; every other quantity is an interval, a displacement. Each rule below falls out of that distinction rather than being special-cased, and each is pinned here because getting one wrong produces a plausible-looking number that is silently wrong.</p>
<div class="step"><span class="name">ambient</span><span class="eq">=</span>20°C</div>
<div class="step"><span class="name">rise</span><span class="eq">=</span>5 K</div>
<p class="prose">A point displaced by an interval is a point.</p>
<div class="step"><span class="name">operating</span><span class="eq">=</span>ambient + rise<span class="eq">=</span><span class="subst">20°C + 5 K</span><span class="eq">=</span><span class="result">298.15 K</span></div>
<p class="prose">The difference of two points is an interval, not a point.</p>
<div class="step"><span class="name">setpoint</span><span class="eq">=</span>15°C</div>
<div class="step"><span class="name">margin</span><span class="eq">=</span>ambient - setpoint<span class="eq">=</span><span class="subst">20°C - 15°C</span><span class="eq">=</span><span class="result">5 K</span></div>
<p class="prose">Conversion is always allowed, in either direction.</p>
<div class="step">ambient<span class="eq">=</span><span class="result">293.15 K</span></div>
<div class="step"><span class="name">freezing</span><span class="eq">=</span>32°F</div>
<div class="step">freezing<span class="eq">=</span><span class="result">0°C</span></div>
<p class="prose">K and °R are absolute, so they are ordinary linear quantities and carry none of the restrictions above.</p>
<div class="step"><span class="name">absolute</span><span class="eq">=</span>293.15 K</div>
<div class="step"><span class="name">doubled</span><span class="eq">=</span>2·absolute<span class="eq">=</span><span class="subst">2·(293.15 K)</span><span class="eq">=</span><span class="result">586.3 K</span></div>
<p class="prose">Intervals on an offset scale still combine freely: it is points that are restricted, not the degree itself.</p>
<div class="step"><span class="name">span</span><span class="eq">=</span>40 K - 10 K<span class="eq">=</span><span class="result">30 K</span></div>
<p class="prose">A thermal calculation, to show the rules are not merely academic.</p>
<div class="step"><span class="name">c_steel</span><span class="eq">=</span>486 J/(kg·K)</div>
<div class="step"><span class="name">mass</span><span class="eq">=</span>12 kg</div>
<div class="step"><span class="name">heat</span><span class="eq">=</span>mass·c_steel·margin<span class="eq">=</span><span class="subst">12 kg·(486 m²·s⁻²·K⁻¹)·(5 K)</span><span class="eq">=</span><span class="result">29160 J</span></div>
<div class="step">heat<span class="eq">=</span><span class="result">29.16 kJ</span></div>

